Large Language Models (LLMs) are inducing a dual transformation in search engines: an internal transformation of search architectures and an external transformation of search traffic dynamics. As part of the internal transformation, LLMs are integrated into search engine pipelines as retrievers, re-rankers, query rewriters, and orchestrators [6]. While yielding strong effectiveness gains, these components introduce substantial computational and infrastructural costs, making search compute-bound and harder to scale. Moreover, LLM-based modules are sensitive to distributional shifts, noisy inputs [6], increasing system performance dependence on data quality, input distributions, and upstream design choices. At the same time, LLMs are emerging as autonomous users of search engines [2, 5], inducing an external transformation of search traffic. Their queries differ from human queries in structure, reformulation patterns, volume, and generation speed, and may be generated iteratively as part of multi-step reasoning processes. However, most search engine components remain optimised for human search behaviours. This mismatch has direct implications for both efficiency and effectiveness: spelling correction and query normalisation may introduce latency without improving results, query understanding components may become less effective, and exact-match caching mechanisms may experience lower hit rates. Together, these transformations challenge the architectural assumptions, optimisation objectives, and effectiveness-efficiency trade-offs of traditional search systems, requiring infrastructures that can meet operational constraints on effectiveness, scalability, latency, and resource efficiency despite higher computational costs, tighter pipeline dependencies, and increasingly heterogeneous query workloads. However, current research predominantly focuses on developing more effective LLM-based retrieval models, while the infrastructural adaptations required to support them in real-world search systems remain comparatively underexplored. Accordingly, this thesis investigates how search infrastructures must adapt to LLMs acting both as search engine components and as search engine users, pursuing two complementary research directions aligned with these transformations.